1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:01,780
Okay, endocrine pathology.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:03,840
So we're going to begin with the pituitary gland.

3
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:06,120
Of course, the pituitary gland is really divided

4
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,600
into an anterior and a posterior pituitary gland.

5
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:10,160
We're going to start with the anterior,

6
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:11,720
and then we'll move to the posterior

7
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:13,200
at the very end of this video.

8
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:15,960
So let's begin with pituitary adenoma.

9
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:19,220
This is a benign tumor of the anterior pituitary cells.

10
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:21,660
Of course, the anterior pituitary cells,

11
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:23,760
they produce a number of different hormones.

12
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,800
If the tumor produces hormones, we call that functional.

13
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,000
And if the tumor does not produce hormones,

14
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,420
we call that non-functional.

15
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:32,340
Now, if the tumor is non-functional,

16
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,160
then it will present with mass effect.

17
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:36,440
And one of the classic presentations would be,

18
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:38,580
for example, bitemporal hemianopsia.

19
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,860
Now, recall that the pituitary sits in the cella tersica,

20
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:44,960
and that adjacent to the cella tersica is the optic chiasm.

21
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:48,120
And so if the tumor was to press on the optic chiasm,

22
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:50,820
this would result in bitemporal hemianopsia,

23
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:53,720
or loss of peripheral vision in both eyes.

24
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:56,580
Just illustrating this, if this is the two eyes here,

25
00:00:56,660 --> 00:00:58,260
and let's pretend they're looking at the numbers

26
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:00,240
one, two, and three, and four here.

27
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:01,800
And when you see the number one,

28
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:04,000
it actually crosses over and then comes like this,

29
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:05,840
and then crosses over the nerve like that.

30
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:07,620
When you would look at the number four,

31
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:09,140
it would come across like this,

32
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:11,080
and then, again, would come down the nerve

33
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:12,020
and cross like that.

34
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:13,580
When you look at the number two,

35
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,920
that would cross over here and come down like this.

36
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:17,540
And when you look at the number three,

37
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:19,600
that would cross over here and come down like this.

38
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:21,720
Now, this here is the optic chiasm.

39
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:23,680
So imagine that if you've got a tumor

40
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:25,420
compressing the optic chiasm here,

41
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:32,440
you're going to lose the ability to see number 1, and you're going to lose the ability to see number 4.

42
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:38,080
And so here you can see that both eyes have lost their peripheral vision, and that's called bitemporal hemianoptia.

43
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:45,140
Now notice that the number 2 never ran through the optic chiasm, and this field also never ran through the optic chiasm.

44
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,380
Another complication could be hypopituitarism.

45
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:57,340
If you have a tumor and it begins to compress the normal pituitary, then you will see signs and symptoms of loss of the hormones that are normally produced by the pituitary,

46
00:01:57,440 --> 00:01:59,400
and that's called hypopituitarism.

47
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:02,800
And finally, a non-functional tumor could also present with headache.

48
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:07,340
Now functioning tumors are going to present with features based on the hormone produced.

49
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:13,880
Recall that the anterior pituitary produces a bunch of hormones, and theoretically, a functional tumor could produce any of these hormones.

50
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:21,920
However, the most commonly produced hormone is prolactin, and hence the most common type of pituitary adenoma is a prolactinoma.

51
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:27,720
Now when you have a prolactinoma, it's going to result in signs and symptoms based on the production of prolactin,

52
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:33,660
and you're well aware of the fact that prolactin is important in the production of milk by the breast, and so you'll get galactorrhea.

53
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:38,620
And in addition, recall that prolactin inhibits GnRH synthesis and release.

54
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:44,260
Now if you don't have GnRH synthesis and release, you're not going to get FSH and LH release,

55
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:50,020
and therefore if you don't get FSH and LH release, that's going to result in amenorrhea in females.

56
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:52,540
So these are the two key findings in females.

57
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:56,460
In males, a prolactinoma would result in decreased libido.

58
00:02:56,620 --> 00:03:03,820
Again, prolactin would inhibit GnRH release, which would then reduce the release of FSH and LH,

59
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:06,260
and so that would result in decreased libido in the male.

60
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:08,960
And at the same time, there could be headache due to mass effect.

61
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:11,720
Now it's important to note that males will not get galactorrhea,

62
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:15,960
and that's because males don't have sufficient breast tissue for milk production.

63
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:20,440
Recall that the functional unit of the breast is called the terminal duct lobular unit.

64
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:23,060
Here's the terminal duct, and here's the lobular unit.

65
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:26,560
Remember that the lobules contain these glands, which basically produce milk.

66
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:29,720
Now this lobular unit would develop in a female after puberty.

67
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:32,500
However, males do not have lobular units in their breast.

68
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:34,280
They simply have terminal ducts.

69
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:37,960
And so because there's only a terminal duct, there's no ability to produce milk.

70
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:40,960
And so again, a male would not develop galactorrhea.

71
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:44,000
Now the treatment of prolactinoma is dopamine agonist.

72
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:48,560
Remember that dopamine from the hypothalamus normally inhibits prolactin secretion.

73
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:53,820
And so if you give a dopamine agonist, you would also be able to inhibit prolactin secretion,

74
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:55,060
and actually the tumor would shrink.

75
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:59,920
And so for example, bromocryptine would be a useful dopamine agonist.

76
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:03,180
In addition, you could also remove the tumor by a surgery.

77
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:06,340
The next step of adenoma is a growth hormone adenoma.

78
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:08,620
Of course, this tumor would release growth hormone.

79
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:12,800
If this tumor were to arise in children, it would result in gigantism.

80
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:16,860
And that's because children have not yet fused their epiphyseal growth plate.

81
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:21,060
And so they would get linear growth of bone in response to the excess growth hormone.

82
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:23,060
Adults develop acromegaly.

83
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:28,240
And acromegaly refers to an enlargement of the bones of the hands, feet, and jaw.

84
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:31,460
In addition, they could grow the visceral organs, for example, the heart.

85
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:34,560
In fact, cardiac failure is the most common cause of death in these patients.

86
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:36,640
They can also develop an enlarged tongue.

87
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:42,120
An important high yield association here is that growth hormone adenoma is often associated with secondary diabetes.

88
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:46,820
Recall from physiology that growth hormone decreases glucose uptake into cells.

89
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:51,160
And so, therefore, if there's a lot of growth hormone around, glucose will not be taken up by cells.

90
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:53,460
And secondary diabetes will develop.

91
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:58,140
The diagnosis of growth hormone adenoma is made by looking at elevated growth hormone levels

92
00:04:58,140 --> 00:05:00,680
and elevated insulin-like growth factor levels.

93
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:04,120
Recall that one of the effects of growth hormone is to go to the liver

94
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:07,200
and cause increased production of insulin-like growth factor.

95
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:11,100
And it's actually the insulin-like growth factor that mediates the growth of tissues.

96
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:15,040
And so, therefore, you would expect insulin-like growth factor to be elevated.

97
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:19,680
Were you to give a patient oral glucose, normally you should be able to suppress growth hormone release.

98
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:25,280
However, oral glucose would not be able to suppress growth hormone if there was a growth hormone adenoma.

99
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:27,380
A potential treatment is octreotide.

100
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:28,800
This is particularly high yield.

101
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:35,180
Recall that growth hormone-releasing hormone actually goes to the anterior pituitary.

102
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:40,900
And in response to growth hormone-releasing hormone, the anterior pituitary then will secrete growth hormone.

103
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:44,920
The way by which octreotide works is that it is a somatostatin analog,

104
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:50,960
and it blocks the response of the anterior pituitary cell to growth hormone-releasing hormone.

105
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:54,580
And so, therefore, you would not be able to generate the production of growth hormone.

106
00:05:55,060 --> 00:05:59,080
You could also give growth hormone receptor antagonists to block the effect of growth hormone,

107
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:01,920
or, of course, surgery could be performed to remove the tumor.

108
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:06,220
Other types of functional adenoma include ACTH cell adenomas.

109
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:09,320
They would secrete ACTH resulting in Cushing syndrome.

110
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:14,860
We'll cover this in more detail when we talk about Cushing syndrome in a later section in this chapter.

111
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:21,220
Of course, there can be a TSH cell adenoma, an LH-producing adenoma, and an FSH-producing adenoma.

112
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:25,160
However, these are rare, and so we won't go into more detail here.

113
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:28,620
Now, shifting gears a little bit, moving to hypopituitarism.

114
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:32,360
This is where you have insufficient production of hormones by the anterior pituitary.

115
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:37,440
Symptoms usually don't arise until you've lost greater than 75% of the pituitary parenchyma.

116
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:40,160
Causes include pituitary adenoma.

117
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:41,860
That would be a common cause in adults.

118
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:45,120
The idea here is that the pituitary adenoma would grow,

119
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:46,980
and it would compress the normal pituitary,

120
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:49,940
which would then cause loss of the tissue of the pituitary.

121
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:54,660
Another possibility with a pituitary adenoma is that you could get bleeding into the adenoma.

122
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:58,060
That's called epoplexy, and when you get bleeding into the adenoma,

123
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:01,500
the adenoma would rapidly grow and would compress the normal tissue.

124
00:07:02,060 --> 00:07:06,220
An additional mechanism of hypopituitarism would be a cranial pharyngioma in children.

125
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:07,660
Again, the same thing.

126
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:10,580
The cranial pharyngioma, which we'll cover when we do CNS,

127
00:07:10,940 --> 00:07:14,220
normally arises within the region of the cellotersica,

128
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:17,300
and so it could compress the normal pituitary tissue.

129
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:20,980
Sheehan syndrome is particularly interesting and particularly high yield.

130
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:24,240
Recall that during pregnancy, there's a high demand of hormone.

131
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:26,060
Due to the physiology of the pregnancy,

132
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:29,020
and so the pituitary gland actually doubles in size.

133
00:07:29,380 --> 00:07:31,560
However, although the gland doubles in size,

134
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:33,580
the blood supply does not increase that much.

135
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:38,120
And so therefore, that leaves the pituitary relatively susceptible to infarction.

136
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:41,920
Now imagine that if a woman gives birth and loses a tremendous amount of blood,

137
00:07:42,260 --> 00:07:44,960
it would be very easy for the pituitary to undergo infarction,

138
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:46,740
and that's called Sheehan syndrome.

139
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:50,640
Now what's interesting about Sheehan syndrome and what's high yield for board examinations

140
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:54,920
is that these patients present with poor lactation and loss of pubic hair.

141
00:07:55,360 --> 00:08:00,520
Loss of pubic hair is a very important association with loss of pituitary function,

142
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:03,120
and that's because pubic hair is dependent on androgens,

143
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:07,720
and those androgens would arise due to LH, which arises from the anterior pituitary.

144
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:10,620
So again, Sheehan syndrome is particularly high yield.

145
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:16,040
Empty cellos syndrome refers to a circumstance in which the cello is empty and the pituitary is missing.

146
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:17,460
It can occur in two ways.

147
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:20,040
It can occur due to secondary trauma to the pituitary,

148
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:22,460
in which case the pituitary would be damaged,

149
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:28,020
or it can occur in a primary circumstance where there is a congenital defect of the cella,

150
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:32,120
such that you get herniation of the arachnoid and the CSF into the cella,

151
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:34,180
which then compresses and destroys the pituitary.

152
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:38,140
Again, the idea here is that if this is the cella with the pituitary sitting in it,

153
00:08:38,380 --> 00:08:42,740
recall that attached to the cella here, the bone of the cella is going to be the dura,

154
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:48,280
and then there's going to be the arachnoid here as a second layer,

155
00:08:48,660 --> 00:08:51,460
and then, of course, you've got the pituitary sitting here,

156
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:54,200
and attached directly to the pituitary would be the pia.

157
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:58,200
Now, if there was herniation of arachnoid and CSF into the cella,

158
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:00,600
that would compress the normal pituitary,

159
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:04,600
and as it compressed the normal pituitary, the pituitary would then be destroyed,

160
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:07,420
resulting in something called empty cella syndrome.

161
00:09:07,420 --> 00:09:11,820
Moving on to the posterior pituitary, recall that there are two hormones

162
00:09:11,820 --> 00:09:14,140
that are released by the posterior pituitary.

163
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:18,640
The first is antidiuretic hormone, or ADH, and the second is oxytocin.

164
00:09:19,020 --> 00:09:22,420
Now, interestingly, neither of these are actually made in the posterior pituitary.

165
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:25,960
Both are made in the hypothalamus and then transmitted via axons

166
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:29,060
to the posterior pituitary for eventual release.

167
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:33,420
Of course, recall from physiology that the role of ADH is to regulate free water

168
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:35,800
at the distal tubules and the collecting ducts.

169
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,540
So, essentially, it controls the movement of free water.

170
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:42,600
And oxytocin mediates uterine contractions and the release of breast milk

171
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:44,180
during the suckling response.

172
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:47,220
The first disorder is central diabetes insipidus.

173
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:52,240
Remember that the word diabetes refers to the fact that someone is urinating too much,

174
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:56,220
and the reason that they're urinating too much is because they have an ADH deficiency.

175
00:09:56,700 --> 00:09:59,380
Now, I mean, just listen to the words, antidiuretic hormone.

176
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:03,360
So, when you have antidiuretic hormone, you will not diurese, you will not urinate.

177
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:07,080
However, if you're lacking antidiuretic hormone, then you will diurese,

178
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:10,080
and you'll get diabetes, which is to urinate too much.

179
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:16,720
Now, this can be due to hypothalamic pathology because, again, ADH is produced in the hypothalamus,

180
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:22,740
or it could be due to posterior pituitary pathology because, again, ADH is released by the posterior pituitary.

181
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:27,580
Some examples of pathology include tumor, trauma, infection, inflammation, etc.

182
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:33,440
Now, the clinical features will be based on the fact that there is a lack of antidiuretic hormone,

183
00:10:33,560 --> 00:10:35,300
so the patient is diuresing too much.

184
00:10:35,380 --> 00:10:36,340
They're urinating too much.

185
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:37,800
And so they'll have polyuria.

186
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:41,100
And in response to that polyuria, they're going to have to drink a lot,

187
00:10:41,180 --> 00:10:45,100
and so they'll also have polydipsia, which means that they're drinking a lot.

188
00:10:45,660 --> 00:10:50,740
Now, the patients will, again, lose a lot of free water because ADH regulates free water.

189
00:10:50,740 --> 00:10:56,060
And so they're going to end up concentrating the sodium in the blood, which is going to give them hypernatremia,

190
00:10:56,180 --> 00:11:00,260
and they're also going to concentrate the serum, giving them a high serum osmolality.

191
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:04,240
Of course, they're losing a ton of free water, so they'll have a low osmolality.

192
00:11:04,420 --> 00:11:07,800
It will be a very dilute urine, and it'll have a low specific gravity.

193
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:10,780
Diagnosis is made by the water deprivation test.

194
00:11:11,060 --> 00:11:13,960
If you force a person to not drink, that should result in dehydration,

195
00:11:14,060 --> 00:11:16,400
which would then result in concentration of the urine.

196
00:11:16,620 --> 00:11:20,680
However, they're not able to concentrate the urine because they have too much antidiuretic hormone,

197
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:25,260
and so, therefore, they will not be able to increase the urine osmolality,

198
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:27,340
i.e., they will not be able to concentrate the urine.

199
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:31,160
The treatment would be to give ADH, and so you can give desmopressin,

200
00:11:31,220 --> 00:11:34,020
which is an ADH analog, which would then reverse the symptoms.

201
00:11:34,660 --> 00:11:38,000
In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, the patients, again, are urinating too much.

202
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:41,780
The problem this time, however, is not in the brain, but instead it's in the kidney,

203
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:44,100
and so they call that nephrogenic diabetes insipidus,

204
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:47,980
and that's because the kidney is not responding to antidiuretic hormone.

205
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:50,780
This is usually due to inherited mutations or drugs.

206
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:54,700
Some classic drugs that can cause this include lithium and demeclocycline,

207
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:57,860
which is an antibiotic that is not used so much anymore.

208
00:11:58,360 --> 00:12:02,080
The symptoms will be similar to central diabetes insipidus.

209
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:04,740
However, there will be no response to desmopressin,

210
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:07,100
so you give desmopressin, which is that ADH analog,

211
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:11,800
and there is no response, and that's because the kidney cannot respond to ADH.

212
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:15,180
The syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion, or SIADH,

213
00:12:15,180 --> 00:12:17,440
is when you have excessive ADH secretion.

214
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:21,320
The clinical features will be based on having too much antidiuretic hormone,

215
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:25,640
so if you have too much antidiuretic hormone, you're not going to diurese.

216
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:28,380
You're going to end up holding free water,

217
00:12:28,520 --> 00:12:32,160
and so therefore the patients get hyponatremia because they dilute out their sodium,

218
00:12:32,820 --> 00:12:35,440
and in addition, they get a low serum osmolality.

219
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:38,360
Patients can also develop mental status change and seizures,

220
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:40,280
and that's because when you have hyponatremia,

221
00:12:40,600 --> 00:12:44,200
that leads to swelling of the nerves and cerebral edema,

222
00:12:44,380 --> 00:12:46,620
which then leads to mental status changes and seizures.

223
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:50,200
The causes include ectopic production,

224
00:12:50,340 --> 00:12:54,720
one of the classic associations, a small cell carcinoma of the lung.

225
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:56,900
Recall that it can produce antidiuretic hormone,

226
00:12:57,340 --> 00:13:01,120
giving you a syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion.

227
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:03,260
A CNS trauma can also do this.

228
00:13:03,580 --> 00:13:06,880
Pulmonary infection, in fact, pulmonary pathology generally can do this.

229
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:09,040
For example, patients with COPD can have this.

230
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:11,620
In addition, patients with pulmonary infection can have this.

231
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:14,760
And then some drugs, for example, cyclophosphamide,

232
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:19,680
can also result in inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion.

233
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:23,040
The treatment is free water restriction, or dameclacycline.

234
00:13:23,420 --> 00:13:27,380
Again, dameclacycline will block the effect of antidiuretic hormone.

235
00:13:27,900 --> 00:13:30,460
And that concludes this section on the pituitary gland.

